    sum: "The world's only Cubist architecture — Prague's faceted crystal facades, prism-philosophy theory and angular furniture of Gočár, Chochol and Janák.",
    desc: "Between 1911 and the First World War, a circle of Czech architects and painters did what no other nation attempted: turned Analytical Cubism into a built national style. Pavel Janák theorised angularity as the Czech spirit against soft Secession curves, while Josef Gočár built the House of the Black Madonna (1912) with faceted bay windows and a famous cubist lamppost, and Josef Chochol composed villa facades like fractured crystal. Painter Emil Filla and sculptor Otto Gutfreund carried the idiom into canvas and relief. Interiors kept cubist furniture, chandeliers and even doorknobs, so the style read as total design. After 1918 the movement softened into rounded Rondo-Cubism for the new republic, but Prague's faceted corners remain unique evidence of a painted avant-garde leaping into masonry.",
