    sum: "Europe's first true porcelain — alchemically cracked at Meissen in 1708, then painted with Höroldt chinoiserie and modelled by Kaendler's lively figures.",
    desc: "Johann Friedrich Böttger, an imprisoned alchemist, and Ehrenfried Tschirnhaus cracked the hard-paste porcelain recipe in 1708 for Augustus the Strong, and the Meissen manufactory opened at Albrechtsburg in 1710 as a state secret. Johann Gregor Höroldt perfected on-glaze enamels for radiant chinoiserie scenes, while Johann Joachim Kändler modelled figurines — commedia players, shepherdesses, the monumental Swan Service — with a liveliness that defined European rococo taste. The blue crossed-swords mark became the first great porcelain signature. As recipes leaked to Vienna and Sèvres, Meissen answered with ever finer modelling and painting; the manufactory still produces by hand today. Its look: brilliant white paste, jewel enamels, gilt edges, and figures caught mid-gesture.",
