    sum: "Ore Mountain woodcraft born of dying mines — nutcrackers, smoking men, candle arches and turning Christmas pyramids in turned and chip-carved pine.",
    desc: "When the Ore Mountains' silver and tin mines declined, miner families turned to wood: Seiffen's turners invented Reifendrehen, slicing figures from a turned ring, and whole villages made toys for Leipzig fairs. The canon grew from mining life — nutcracker kings and gendarmes, Räuchermännchen figures that puff incense smoke, the Schwibbogen candle arch echoing a mine-tunnel cross-section, and carousel-like Christmas pyramids whose fan-blades rise over hearth heat. Palettes are frank: wax-reds, forest greens, cream paint over pale wood, with white-flocked snow textures. Trade catalogues and German emigration carried the craft worldwide, and workshops in Seiffen still hand-carve and turn. It is mining cosmology domesticated: the pit, the watchman and the light, miniaturised for the parlour.",
