    sum: "Saxon colonists' churches walled into village fortresses — onion-buttressed rings, storage chambers and watchtowers above apricot-orchard villages.",
    desc: "Transylvanian Saxons, colonists invited from the 12th century, rebuilt their village churches as fortresses against raids: concentric curtain walls, massive ring walls with grain chambers, and towers where each guild kept a floor. Prejmer's walls could shelter whole villages, while Biertan's triple ring and its celebrated multi-tumbler sacristy lock made it the see of the Saxon bishops. The aesthetic is stern mineral harmony: ochre and white plaster, dark shingle roofs, catwalks under tiled slopes, all over green hill orchards. After Saxon emigration in the 1990s many villages emptied, and trusts led by patrons including King Charles III now restore roofs and murals. UNESCO lists seven village ensembles, with Viscri and Saschiz among the best known.",
