    sum: "Bulgaria's furred and belled winter mummers — horned wooden masks, goatskin suits and huge copper chan bells dancing evil out of the village.",
    desc: "Kukeri are masked men who, between Christmas and Lent, clothe themselves in shaggy goatskin and sheep-fleece suits, wear carved wooden masks with horns, beaks and painted sun-discs, and strap belts of giant copper chan bells that announce them a kilometre away. Dancing in deep, sweeping lunges to shake the earth awake and frighten evil spirits, they echo Thracian Dionysian rites recorded on antiquity's reliefs. Regional styles differ: Shiroka Laka's Rhodope masks run to feathers and painted ribbons, while Pernik's Surva festival — held since 1966 — gathers thousands of masked performers from across the Balkans in competitive boulevards of fur and bronze sound. Costumes are made by families and local clubs, maintained for decades. The aesthetic is overwhelming: black wool pile, rawhide, ochre-red painted wood, and thunder as ornament.",
