    sum: "Karst field-clearing made visible — miles of stacked gromače walls, shepherds' bunja shelters and terraced olive gardens of the Dalmatian islands.",
    desc: "Dalmatia's limestone karst yielded nothing until farmers moved the stone: clearing fields produced gromače — dry-stacked walls a metre or more high — running in grid and terrace across Hvar, Brač and the mainland, no mortar in any joint. Shepherds roofed the leftover technique into bunja and kažun shelters, beehive huts with corbelled domes, while hillside terraces held olives and vines against wind and runoff. On Hvar, the Stari Grad Plain still shows Greek parcellation walled in this fashion since the fourth century BC, and Brač's quarries fed Diocletian's Palace with the same white stone. UNESCO inscribed the Art of Dry Stone Walling (Croatia among eight states) in 2018, and volunteer camps now rebuild collapsed stretches. The look is silver-grey geometry: wall and shadow, terraced light, sea beyond.",
