    sum: "Lisbon's hand-cut limestone pavements — black and white waves, ships and gulls laid shard by shard by calceteiros since the Rossio set the canon.",
    desc: "Calçada Portuguesa turns the sidewalk into intarsia: irregular shards of white limestone and black basalt are hand-laid by calceteiros into waves, armillary spheres, ships and fish. The canon was set in the 1840s when the Rossio square was paved with its rolling mar-largo wave pattern, and Lisbon's guilds have trained apprentices ever since, sketching with chalk outlines directly on screed. The craft spread across the Portuguese empire — Macau's wavy hillsides and Rio's Copacabana boardwalk carry it. Its vices are real: marble-hard, slippery in rain, and costly to maintain, prompting debates about replacing it, while the municipality defends it as intangible heritage and the city's signature. Up close it is a mosaic of thousands ofhammered facets; from a window it is drawing on the ground.",
