    desc: "Pelota basque is less one sport than a family of wall games played against the fronton — one-, two- or three-walled courts built into church squares and town halls across the Basque Country. Modalities range from bare-hand pelota, where palm meets goatskin ball with a slap like a gunshot, to cesta punta or jai alai, where a long curved wicker cesta slings the ball at speeds ranked among the fastest of any ball sport. The fronton itself is village architecture: painted angle walls with sponsor murals, seating steps facing the play wall, kids practising between matches. The game exported early — Miami jai alai frontons and Manila courts spread the vocabulary. Its aesthetic is stark and kinetic: chalk-white walls, ochre clay, green shirts, black helmets, and the blur of the pelotari's leap.",
