    sum: "Brittany's walled sacred compounds — triumphal arch gate, ossuary and narrative calvaries where granite and painted saints crowd the whole Passion.",
    desc: "In western Brittany, proud rural parishes rebuilt their churches within enclosed compounds — the enclos paroissiaux — entered through a triumphal Roman arch and containing an ossuary, a funeral charniers' porch, and above all a calvary: a granite tree crowded with carved Passion scenes, from Guimiliau's 1580s masterpiece with its narrative crowds and lampooned rich man in hell, to Saint-Thégonnec's staged multi-figure version. Beaumanoir-family masons set Renaissance detail against late-Gothic bones, while local painters layered saints' statues in gilded polychrome inside. The closes express a peculiar Breton theology: communal, tactile, unafraid of death's furniture, funded by linen trade wealth. Over seventy survive, and their grey granite glows under Atlantic rain. They remain among Europe's most distinctive sacred landscapes.",
