    o: "Marseille and Provence", g: "Provence; exported collections",
    p: "1790s onward", era: "post-Revolution folk sacred",
    sum: "Provence's clay little saints — the ravi, the blind man and the whole village crowding the crèche since the Revolution closed the churches.",
    desc: "When Revolutionary law emptied the churches, Marseillais Louis Lagnel began moulding small clay figures around 1797 so families could stage nativity plays at home — and santons, little saints, became Provence's own folk art. The canon fills the crèche with the whole Midi: the ravi (the delighted one), the blind man regaining sight, the miller, the Arlésienne, the tambourinaire with his galoubet, shepherds and hens in pale Provençal colours. Fired in molds from Aubagne and Marseille clay, then hand-painted, santons gained their own annual fair on Marseille's Canebière from 1803, still running each December. Museums in Marseille and workshops in Aubagne keep the canon and the painters' styles distinct. It is folk theology as village portrait: everyone earns a place at the stable.",
