    o: "Limoges, France", g: "churches and treasuries across medieval Europe",
    p: "12th-15th centuries (champlevé); painted enamel after 1470", era: "Gothic craft",
    sum: "Medieval Europe's enamel factory — copper chasses and Becket caskets with jewel-blue grounds and gilt figures, exported by the thousand from Limoges.",
    desc: "From the twelfth century, Limoges workshops carved troughs into copper sheets, filled them with powdered glass, fired, polished and gilded, producing champlevé reliquary chasses, altar crosses and book covers that shipped across Latin Christendom. Their aesthetic is instantly legible: intense cobalt and turquoise grounds against gilt figures with punched stipple halos, flat hieratic bodies, and wheel-cut leaf scrolls. Dozens of caskets depicting the murder of Thomas Becket survive from a single commercial canon — medieval Europe's mass-market devotional art. In the late fifteenth century the city pivoted to painted enamels, with court portraitists like Léonard Limosin working in grisaille and jewel tones on plaques. The V&A and British Museum hold the great collections, and Limoges kept enamel as a living trade.",
    col: [{ h: "#1f4e9c", n: "enamel cobalt" }, { h: "#3aa6a0", n: "turquoise cell" }, { h: "#c8a02c", n: "gilt figure" }, { h: "#7a3b8f", n: "purple enamel" }, { h: "#b87333", n: "copper ground" }],
