    p: "14th century roots; 1665 manufacture royale; UNESCO 2009", era: "early modern to modern craft",
    sum: "The Creuse valley's flat-woven tapestry tradition — forest verdures, grotesques and Lurçat's modern revival woven on low-warp looms in workshops on both banks.",
    desc: "In the valley of the Creuse, dyers and weavers built Aubusson and Felletin into France's great provincial tapestry centre: low-warp looms weaving upside-down from behind, wool weft over linen warp, producing large flat-woven room-size hangings. Sixteenth-century verdures — dense forests where nobles hunted among monogrammed trees — set a taste that persisted into millefleur borders and later pastoral and exotic scenes; in 1665 royal letters patent gave the town manufacture status. Mid-twentieth-century Jean Lurçat's angular, boldly coloured designs revived the workshops for modern interiors, and cartoon-based weaving continues under the Cité internationale de la tapisserie. UNESCO inscribed the craft in 2009 as living heritage. The look: vegetable-dye greens and madder reds, woven shadow, and edges where forest, cloud and thread meet.",
    col: [{ h: "#3f5d3a", n: "verdure green" }, { h: "#8a3b2a", n: "madder red" }, { h: "#c9b072", n: "gold ochre" }, { h: "#2f4a6b", n: "woad blue" }, { h: "#d9c7a0", n: "linen ground" }],
